<p>This despite the fact that few of those merchants or travelers crossed the Silk Road’s entire length. But we can all name at least one man who made the full journey: the thirteenth-century Venetian explorer Marco Polo, who as a teenager first made the trek to Kublai Khan’s pleasure dome with his father. (At that point, the Mongol Empire had already unified its routes, but not before laying waste to a fair few settlements along the way.) <span style="font-size: 13pt;">It is to Polo’s writings that we owe our enduringly romantic image of the Silk Road, but he understood the dangers of that lawless gauntlet across empires as well as anyone. </span><a style="font-size: 13pt;" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HK0TevewmbQ">The Urban Atlas video</a><span style="font-size: 13pt;"> above lists a few of them, including desert heat, diseases, hallucinations, banditry, and slavery.&nbsp;</span>Today, we encounter no such perils on the way from&nbsp;Rome to&nbsp;Xi’an,&nbsp;a journey we can make on a&nbsp;flight with a stopover in Beijing. Perhaps Marco Polo would say we’ve all gone soft, but then,&nbsp;he’d probably be more interested in learning about our&nbsp;roads in the sky.</p>
